+-----------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------

PAM configuration
=================

sudo-rs always authenticates through PAM. OpenBSD has no PAM in the base
system, so security/openpam must be installed and /etc/pam.d/sudo must
exist before sudo can be used. The sudoers file alone is not enough.

Write a service that performs a real authentication check for the
invoking user. security/openpam provides pam_bsdauth(8) for OpenBSD's BSD
authentication. A minimal service is:

    auth    required /usr/local/lib/security/pam_bsdauth.so
    account required /usr/local/lib/security/pam_permit.so
    session required /usr/local/lib/security/pam_permit.so

Do not use pam_permit(8), pam_self(8) or pam_rootok(8) as the only
authentication module: they accept the user without verifying a password
or other credential. Review the configured stack with pamtester(1) from
security/openpam before relying on it.

Privilege model
===============

This package installs sudo(8) setuid root, matching security/sudo. Any
defect in the porting patches, the PAM stack or the sudoers policy can
therefore yield root privileges. The OpenBSD patch set is a first porting
attempt: build and exercise the escalation, sudoedit, timestamp and
terminal checks on a dedicated machine before deployment. Until then,
prefer the packages already used on the target system.

The default descriptor policy marks inherited descriptors close-on-exec;
it does not close descriptors that the exec error pipe or an askpass
helper still needs before exec succeeds.
